apmo-2010-03.body:
Fix a person $P$ with $d$ acquaintances; let $S$ be the set of them and $T$ the remaining $n-1-d$ people (other than $P$), and let $e$ be the number of acquainted pairs between $S$ and $T$. Bound the counted pairs:
- pairs $\{P,x\}$: $x\in T$ must know someone in $S$, so at most $e$;
- pairs inside $S$: at most $\binom d2$;
- pairs inside $T$: at most $\binom{n-1-d}2$;
- pairs between $S$ and $T$: at most $d(n-1-d)-e$ (the $e$ acquainted ones are not counted).

The total is at most $\binom d2+\binom{n-1-d}2+d(n-1-d)=\binom{n-1}2$, independent of $d$ and $e$. With the star example, the maximum is $\binom{n-1}2$.
